{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","count":14,"questions":[{"id":52526,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A neutron star with magnetic moment of magnitude $\\mathrm{m}$ is spinning with angular velocity $\\omega$ about its magnetic axis. The electromagnetic power P radiated by it is given by $\\mu_{0}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{m}^{\\mathrm{y}} \\omega^{\\mathrm{z}} \\mathrm{c}^{\\mathrm{u}}$ where $\\mu_{0}$ and $\\mathrm{c}$ are the permeability and speed of light in free space, respectively. Then","options":["$x=1, y=2, z=4$ and $u=-3$","$x=1, y=2, z=4$ and $u=3$","$x=-1, y=2, z=4$ and $u=-3$","$x=-1, y=2, z=4$ and $u=3$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\mathrm{P}=\\left[\\mathrm{M} \\mathrm{L}^{2} \\mathrm{~T}^{-3}\\right]\\mu_{0}=\\left[\\mathrm{M} \\mathrm{L} \\mathrm{T}^{-2} \\mathrm{I}^{-2}\\right]\\mathrm{m}=\\left[\\mathrm{I} \\mathrm{L}^{2}\\right]\\omega=\\left[\\mathrm{T}^{-1}\\right]\\mathrm{C}=\\left[\\mathrm{L} \\mathrm{T}^{-1}\\right]\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{M} \\mathrm{L}^{2} \\mathrm{~T}^{-3}\\right]=\\left[\\mathrm{M} \\mathrm{L} \\mathrm{T}^{-2} \\mathrm{I}^{-2}\\right]^{\\mathrm{x}}\\left[\\mathrm{I} \\mathrm{L}^{2}\\right]^{\\mathrm{y}}\\left[\\mathrm{T}^{-1}\\right]^{\\mathrm{z}}\\left[\\mathrm{LT}^{-1}\\right]^{\\mathrm{l}}\\mathrm{x}=1, \\mathrm{y}=2, \\mathrm{z}=4, \\mathrm{u}=-3$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2131f52882840ca2d50"},{"id":52527,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The potential energy of a point particle is given by the expression $\\mathrm{V}(\\mathrm{x})=-\\alpha \\mathrm{x}+\\beta \\sin (\\mathrm{x} / \\gamma)$. A dimensionless combination of the constant $\\alpha, \\beta$ and $\\gamma$ is-","options":["$\\alpha / \\beta \\gamma$","$\\alpha^{2} / \\beta \\gamma$","$\\gamma / \\alpha \\beta$","$\\alpha \\gamma / \\beta$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\begin{array}{l}\n{[\\alpha]=\\mathrm{MLT}^{-2}} \\\\\n{[\\beta]=\\mathrm{ML}^{2} \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}} \\\\\n{[\\gamma]=\\mathrm{L}}\n\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213593cfd36d168ed3d"},{"id":52528,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A massive black hole of mass $m$ and radius $R$ is spinning with angular velocity $\\omega .$ The power $\\mathrm{P}$ radiated by it as gravitational waves is given by $\\mathrm{P}=\\mathrm{Gc}^{-5} \\mathrm{~m}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{R}^{\\mathrm{y}} \\omega^{\\mathrm{z}}$, where $\\mathrm{c}$ and $\\mathrm{G}$ are speed of light in free space, and the universal gravitational constant, respectively. Then","options":["$x=-1, y=2, z=4$","$x=1, y=1, z=4$","$x=-1, y=4, z=4$","$x=2, y=4, z=6$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\mathrm{P}=\\mathrm{ML}^{2} \\mathrm{~T}^{-3}, \\mathrm{c}=\\mathrm{LT}^{-1}, \\omega=\\mathrm{T}^{-1}, \\mathrm{R}=\\mathrm{L}, \\mathrm{m}=\\mathrm{M}\\mathrm{G}=\\mathrm{M}^{-1} \\mathrm{~L}^{3} \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^{2} \\mathrm{~T}^{-3}\\right]=\\left[\\mathrm{M}^{-1} \\mathrm{~L}^{3} \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{LT}^{-1}\\right]^{-5} \\mathrm{M}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{L}^{\\mathrm{y}} \\mathrm{T}^{-\\mathrm{z}}$\nsolve we get $\\mathrm{x}=2, \\mathrm{y}=4, \\mathrm{z}=6$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21346b09a5b8acb67be"},{"id":52529,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The dimensions of stefan-Boltzmann constant $\\sigma$ can be written in terms of Planck's constanth' Boltzmann constant $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{B}}$ and the speed of light $\\mathrm{c}$ as $\\sigma=\\mathrm{h}^{\\alpha} \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{B}}{ }^{\\beta} \\mathrm{C}^{\\gamma} .$ Here","options":["$\\alpha=3, \\beta=4$ and $\\gamma=-3$","$\\alpha=3, \\beta=-4$ and $\\gamma=2$","$\\alpha=-3, \\beta=4$ and $\\gamma=-2$","$\\alpha=2, \\beta=-3$ and $\\gamma=-1$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\quad\\left(\\mathrm{ML}^{\\circ} \\mathrm{T}^{-2} \\theta^{-4}\\right)=\\left(\\mathrm{ML}^{2} \\mathrm{~T}^{-1}\\right)^{\\gamma}\\left(\\mathrm{ML}^{2} \\mathrm{~T}^{-2} \\theta^{-1}\\right)^{\\beta}\\left(\\mathrm{LT}^{-1}\\right)^{\\gamma}$\nSolving we get","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213021632a3c78ef60a"},{"id":52530,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The time period of a body undergoing simple harmonic motion is given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi></math> is the pressure, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>D</mi></math> is density and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math> is surface tension. The values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi></math> respectively are","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>'</mo></msup></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mtable columnalign=\"left\"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfenced></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>'</mo></msup></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math><br />\nOn solving<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da757cf212e58f6015d42"},{"id":52531,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Four students measure the height of a tower. Each student uses a different method and each measures the height many different times. The data for each are plotted below. The measurement with highest precision is\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F-ptR8QyH9oKF4zuOeNQP63Wj1Vg11El8yEU30DkcAiw.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F-ptR8QyH9oKF4zuOeNQP63Wj1Vg11El8yEU30DkcAiw.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F-ptR8QyH9oKF4zuOeNQP63Wj1Vg11El8yEU30DkcAiw.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["I","II","III","IV"],"answer":0,"solution":"Precision mean every time reading is coming nearly same.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2130e39c98ddb757c7a"},{"id":52532,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The Quantum Hall Resistance $\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{H}}$ is a fundamental constant with dimensions of resistance. If $\\mathrm{h}$ is Planck's constant and e the electron charge, then the dimension of $\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{H}}$ is the same as -","options":["$\\mathrm{e}^{2} / \\mathrm{h}$","$\\mathrm{h} / \\mathrm{e}^{2}$","$\\mathrm{h}^{2} / \\mathrm{e}$","$\\mathrm{e} / \\mathrm{h}^{2}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{R}=\\frac{\\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{i}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{V} \\times \\mathrm{i}}{\\mathrm{i}^{2}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{\\mathrm{i}^{2}}$\nenergy $=\\mathrm{h} \\mathrm{v}=\\frac{\\mathrm{h}}{\\mathrm{t}}$\nPower $=\\frac{\\text { energy }}{\\mathrm{t}}\\mathrm{P}=\\frac{\\mathrm{h}}{\\mathrm{t}^{2}}\\mathrm{i}=\\frac{\\mathrm{e}}{\\mathrm{t}}\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{\\mathrm{i}^{2}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{h}}{\\mathrm{e}^{2}}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21337839e55aa5fd904"},{"id":52533,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A liquid drop placed on a horizontal plane has a near spherical shape (slightly flattened due to gravity). Let $\\mathrm{R}$ be the radius of its largest horizontal section. A small disturbance causes the drop to vibrate with frequency $\\mathrm{v}$ about its equilibrium shape. By dimensional analysis the ratio $\\frac{\\mathrm{v}}{\\sqrt{\\sigma / \\rho \\mathrm{R}^{3}}}$ can be (Here $\\sigma$ is surface tension, $\\rho$ is density, $\\mathrm{g}$ is acceleration due to gravity, and $\\mathrm{k}$ is arbitrary dimensionless constant)-","options":["$\\mathrm{k} \\rho \\mathrm{gR}^{2} / \\sigma$","$\\mathrm{k} \\rho \\mathrm{R}^{2} / \\mathrm{g} \\sigma$","$\\mathrm{k} \\rho \\mathrm{R}^{3} / \\mathrm{g} \\sigma$","$\\mathrm{k} \\rho / \\mathrm{g} \\sigma$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\frac{\\mathrm{v}}{\\sqrt{\\sigma / \\rho \\mathrm{R}^{3}}}$ is dimensionless $\\mathrm{k} \\rho \\mathrm{gR}^{2} / \\sigma$ is also dimensionless","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213776c9acabfb8d50d"},{"id":52534,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Using dimensional analysis the resistivity in terms of fundamental constants $\\mathrm{h}, \\mathrm{m}_{\\mathrm{e}}, \\mathrm{c}, \\mathrm{e}, \\varepsilon_{0}$ can be expressed as","options":["$\\frac{\\mathrm{h}}{\\varepsilon_{0} \\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{e}} \\mathrm{ce}^{2}}$","$\\frac{\\varepsilon_{0} \\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{e}} \\mathrm{ce}^{2}}{\\mathrm{~h}}$","$\\frac{\\mathrm{h}^{2}}{\\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{e}} \\mathrm{ce}^{2}}$","$\\frac{\\mathrm{m}_{\\mathrm{e}} \\varepsilon_{0}}{\\mathrm{ce}^{2}}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\rho=\\frac{E}{J}E=\\left[M L T^{-3} I^{-1}\\right]\\mathrm{J}=\\left[\\mathrm{I} \\mathrm{L}^{-2}\\right]\\mathrm{h}=\\mathrm{M} \\mathrm{L}^{2} \\mathrm{~T}^{-1}\\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{e}}=\\mathrm{M}\\mathrm{c}=\\mathrm{LT}^{-1}\\varepsilon_{0}=\\mathrm{M}^{-1} \\mathrm{~L}^{-3} \\mathrm{~T}^{4} \\mathrm{I}^{2}\\therefore \\rho=\\frac{\\mathrm{h}^{2}}{\\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{e}} \\mathrm{ce}^{2}}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2136519e762e93b3c55"},{"id":52535,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"If the current through a resistor in a circuit increases by $3 \\%$, the power dissipated by the resistor","options":["increases approximately by $3 \\%$","increases approximately by $6 \\%$","increases approximately by $9 \\%$","decreases approximately by $3 \\%$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{P=I^{2} R}\\mathrm{\\frac{\\Delta P}{P}=\\frac{2 \\Delta I}{I}=6 \\%}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213bfabef99fd69adad"},{"id":52536,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Assume that, the drag force on a football depends only on the density of the air, velocity of the ball and the cross-sectional area of the ball. Balls of different sizes but the same density are dropped in an air column. The terminal velocity reached by balls of masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>250</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>125</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> are in the ratio","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>Given, drag force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></math> depends on air density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>σ</mi></math>, velocity of ball <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math> and area of cross-section of ball <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>.<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>Substituting dimensions of different physical quantities, we have<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>L</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced><mi>a</mi></msup><msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced><mi>b</mi></msup><msup><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mo> </mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mi>c</mi></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>Equating dimensions of fundamental quantities, we have</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mi>ii</mi></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mi>iii</mi></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p>Putting the values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi></math> in Eq (ii), we get</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∵</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>So, drag force on football is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>·</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>·</mo><mi>A</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>When football reaches terminal speed, its weight is balanced by drag force.</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>σ</mi><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>A</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>where, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo></math>terminal speed of football.</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>σ</mi><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>σ</mi><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mfenced><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>[ from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>ρ</mi></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo></math> density of football]</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mfenced><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><mfrac><mn>250</mn><mn>125</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6b67c2d92f22c3cb464"},{"id":52537,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The dimensions of the area A of a black hole can be written in terms of the universal constant $\\mathrm{G}$, its mass $\\mathrm{M}$ and the speed of light $\\mathrm{c}$ as $A=G^{\\alpha} M^{\\beta} c^{7} .$ Here","options":["$\\alpha=-2, \\beta=-2$, and $\\gamma=4$","$\\alpha=2, \\beta=2$, and $\\gamma=-4$","$\\alpha=3, \\beta=3$, and $\\gamma=-2$","$\\alpha=-3, \\beta=-3$, and $\\gamma=2$"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fvq7RPwY0Y0na84eDvAWnqryVYOMleTCojbk_yu9xU4I.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fvq7RPwY0Y0na84eDvAWnqryVYOMleTCojbk_yu9xU4I.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fvq7RPwY0Y0na84eDvAWnqryVYOMleTCojbk_yu9xU4I.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213d38d4a254bf38b44"},{"id":52538,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Stoke's law states that the viscous drag force $F$ experienced by a sphere of radius a, moving with a speed $V$ through a fluid with coefficient of viscosity $\\eta$, is given by $F=6$ inav. If this fluid is flowing through a cylindrical pipe of radiusr, leng th $/$ and a pressure difference of Pacross its two ends, then the volume of water $V$ which\nflows through the pipe in time $t$ can be written as $\\frac{V}{t}=k\\left(\\frac{P}{\\ell}\\right)^{a} \\eta^{b} r^{c}$, where $k$ is a dimensional constant. Correct values of $a, b$ and $c$ are","options":["$a=1, b=-1, c=4$","$a=-1, b=1, c=4$","$a=2, b=-1, c=3$","$a=1, b=-2, c=-4$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\frac{V}{t}=k\\left(\\frac{P}{\\ell}\\right)^{a} \\cdot \\eta^{b} \\cdot \\gamma^{c} \\\\\n{[r]=L ;[l]=L} \\\\\n{[P]=M L^{-1} T^{-2} ;[\\eta]=M L^{-1} T^{-1}} \\\\\n{[\\mathrm{~V}]=L^{3}} \\\\\n{[t]=T}\n\\end{array}\nBy calculation\n$a=1 ; b=-1 ; c=4","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SA)","_marksId":"6174d213031ba889e8097b5d"},{"id":52539,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Units and Dimensions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Young-Laplace law states that the excess pressure inside a soap bubble of radius $\\mathrm{R}$ is given by $\\Delta \\mathrm{P}=4 \\sigma / \\mathrm{R}$ where $\\sigma$ is the coefficient of surface tension of the soap. The Eötvös number $E_{0}$ is a dimensionless number that is used to describe the shape of bubbles rising through a surrounding fluid. It is a combination of $\\mathrm{g}$, the acceleration due to gravity, $\\rho$, the density of the surrounding fluid, $\\sigma$ and a characterstic length scale $L$ which could be the radius of the bubble. A possible expression for $E_{0}$ is","options":["$\\frac{\\rho \\mathrm{g}}{\\sigma \\mathrm{L}^{3}}$","$\\frac{\\rho L^{2}}{\\sigma g}$","$\\frac{\\rho g L^{2}}{\\sigma}$","$\\frac{\\mathrm{gL}^{2}}{\\sigma \\rho}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"Check dimensionally","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2133f8faadadda70d9b"}]}